# bus_resolve.ys -- shared-bus tri-state resolution pre-pass.
#
# Run on the freshly GHDL-imported design, AFTER `hierarchy`/`proc` and
# BEFORE `synth_ecp5`. Paths are relative to fpga/ (yosys `script` does not
# change directory); both fpga/Makefile's `bit` rule and
# docs/notes/test_netlist_integrity.py invoke THIS file, so the sequence has
# exactly one definition.
#
# Without this pre-pass, synth_ecp5's own coarse stage (`flatten;
# tribuf -logic; ...`) leaves 24 multiply-driven nets -- the 16-bit M bus at
# mar U54/U59 and the 8-bit MDR bus at mdr U18 -- and nextpnr-ecp5 aborts at
# placement with "Net 'dino_core_i.m[0]' is multiply driven by cell ports".
# Two independent mechanisms, both rooted in how ghdl-yosys-plugin hands a
# VHDL resolved signal to yosys (it emits one `connect` per driver and leaves
# the arbitration to `tribuf`):
#
#   1. VECTOR-WIDE vs BIT-WIDE tri-states never merge. `tribuf` groups
#      tri-state drivers by the WHOLE SigSpec of their Y port, so the ONE
#      8-bit-wide $tribuf that ttl_at28c256's `d_i <= q when ... else
#      (others => 'Z')` becomes is a different dict key from the eight 1-bit
#      $tribufs of the '245 sharing those same bits. Each group is then
#      resolved INDEPENDENTLY and both results are connected to the same
#      bits. `simplemap t:$tribuf` splits every vector $tribuf into per-bit
#      $_TBUF_ first, so one net == one group.
#
#   2. PROVABLY-DEAD tri-state drivers are not removed. See
#      synth/dead_tbuf.v for the full explanation -- constant-direction '245s
#      leave an E=1'0 $_TBUF_ on a net whose real driver ('193 counter Q,
#      registered ROM data) is not a tri-state at all and is therefore
#      invisible to `tribuf -logic`.
#
# Both steps are exact, not heuristic: (1) is a pure re-grouping of the same
# drivers, (2) removes cells that can never drive. Machine semantics are
# unchanged -- the microcode's one-hot bus policing (microcode_gen.check_word)
# still guarantees at most one enabled driver per bus per T-state, which is
# what makes the resulting mux resolution equivalent to the board's wired-OR.
#
# synth_ecp5's coarse stage re-runs `flatten` (a no-op on an already-flat
# design) and `tribuf -logic` (a no-op once this file has left zero tri-state
# cells behind), so nothing here is undone or duplicated downstream.

flatten
opt_expr
simplemap t:$tribuf
opt_expr
techmap -map synth/dead_tbuf.v
tribuf -logic
check
